# `actuarial::mortality::gompertz`

Builds a mortality law from a Gompertz force.

## Signature

```cpp
actuarial::Mortality actuarial::mortality::gompertz(const pluss::Series &attained_ages, double b, double c);
actuarial::MortalityModel actuarial::mortality::gompertz(double b, double c);
```

## What It Does

The Gompertz force of mortality is {math}`\mu_x=b\,c^{x}` with `b ≥ 0` and `c > 1`: it grows by the factor `c` every year of age. The law over the attained ages `x` has {math}`q_x=1-\exp(-\mu_x)`. The first overload evaluates the law at the given ages and returns the `Mortality`. The second binds `b` and `c` into a [`MortalityModel`](MortalityModel.md) to be applied to many age vectors. Use Gompertz for adult ages, where the exponential growth of mortality is a good description; [`makeham`](makeham.md) adds an age-independent term for accidents.

## Parameters

`attained_ages`
: One-dimensional, finite, non-negative ages at the start of each year.

`b`
: Level of the force at age 0, 0 or more.

`c`
: Yearly growth factor of the force, greater than 1.

## Returns

A `Mortality` of one probability per age, or a `MortalityModel` that produces one when called with ages.

## Errors

Throws `gompertz_force: b must be >= 0`, `gompertz_force: c must be > 1 for Gompertz-style age acceleration`, `gompertz_force: attained_ages must be >= 0` and `... must be 1D`. Calling an undefined `MortalityModel` throws `actuarial Operator is undefined`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a law at four ages

Gompertz mortality from age 60: probabilities rise each year.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Mortality m = actuarial::mortality::gompertz(pluss::make_series({60.0, 61.0, 62.0, 63.0}), 0.0001, 1.08);
  out.writeln("qx    =", m.qx().items<double>());
  out.writeln("force =", m.force().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: the force doubles every ln 2 / ln c years

With c = 1.08 the force doubles in about 9 years.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Mortality m = actuarial::mortality::gompertz(pluss::make_series({60.0, 69.0}), 0.0001, 1.08);
  const auto mu = m.force().items<double>();
  out.writeln("force at 69 / force at 60 =", mu[1] / mu[0], " c^9 =", std::pow(1.08, 9.0));
}
```

### Example 3: a reusable model

Bind the parameters once and call the model with different ages.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::MortalityModel model = actuarial::mortality::gompertz(0.0001, 1.08);
  out.writeln("key:", model.key());
  out.writeln("from 60:", model(pluss::make_series({60.0, 61.0})).qx().items<double>());
  out.writeln("from 70:", model(pluss::make_series({70.0, 71.0})).qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: stronger acceleration

A larger c makes the old ages much more dangerous.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series ages = pluss::make_series({60.0, 70.0, 80.0, 90.0});
  out.writeln("c = 1.08:", actuarial::mortality::gompertz(ages, 0.0001, 1.08).qx().items<double>());
  out.writeln("c = 1.10:", actuarial::mortality::gompertz(ages, 0.0001, 1.10).qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: the errors

Parameters outside their range are refused, and so is an undefined model.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::mortality::gompertz(pluss::make_series({60.0, 61.0, 62.0, 63.0}), -0.1, 1.08); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::mortality::gompertz(pluss::make_series({60.0, 61.0, 62.0, 63.0}), 0.0001, 1.0); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::mortality::gompertz(pluss::make_series({-1.0}), 0.0001, 1.08); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::MortalityModel{}(pluss::make_series({60.0, 61.0, 62.0, 63.0})); });
}
```

